<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edgar Degas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hilaire-Germain-Edgar De Gas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edgar Degas was a French artist known for his pastels and oil paintings, more than half of his works depicting dancers. Trained at the École des Beaux-Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he first meant to be a history painter, then brought its methods to contemporary subjects such as ballet dancers, bathing women, racehorses and jockeys. Regarded as a founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a>, he helped organise the eight Impressionist exhibitions of 1874–1886 and showed in all but one, yet he rejected the term and preferred to be called a realist. The Dreyfus Affair intensified his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a>; after 1890 his eyesight worsened, and he apparently stopped working in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edgar-degas.jpg" alt="Edgar Degas" width="330" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgar Degas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edgar_Degas_self_portrait_1855FXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Degas was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 19 July 1834, the oldest of five children of the banker Augustin De Gas and Célestine Musson De Gas, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>. He adopted the less grandiose spelling Degas as an adult. His mother died when he was thirteen. After a baccalauréat in literature in 1853 he registered as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> copyist, though his father expected him to study law, and he enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> with little effort. In 1855 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-auguste-dominique-ingres">Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres</a>, whose advice to draw lines and still more lines he never forgot, and he was admitted to the École des Beaux-Arts. From July 1856 he spent three years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> copying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> masters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1859 he began The Bellelli Family, unfinished until 1867, and several history paintings, among them Sémiramis Building Babylon and Young Spartans Exercising. He first exhibited at the Salon in 1865. Within a few years, influenced chiefly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edouard-manet">Édouard Manet</a>, whom he had met in 1864, he turned to contemporary subjects. In 1870 he enlisted in the National Guard for the defence of Paris, and a rifle test showed that his eyesight was defective, a worry for the rest of his life. A Cotton Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, painted there from 1872, was the only work a museum bought in his lifetime.</p><p>In 1877 Degas invited Mary Cassatt to join the third Impressionist exhibition, and they worked closely on a planned prints journal in 1879 and 1880 until he withdrew from it. His pastel nudes at the eighth exhibition in 1886 drew the most critical writing on him in his lifetime, most of it favourable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Degas rejected painting outdoors and denied that his art was spontaneous. He said that nothing in it was less so, and that it came from reflection and study of the great masters. He is nonetheless described more accurately as an Impressionist than as a member of any other movement. His one sculpture shown in his lifetime, The Little Dancer of Fourteen Years, a nearly life-size wax with real hair and a cloth tutu, appeared in 1881. Critics found its realism exceptional but many called it ugly. His heirs found about 150 wax sculptures in his studio, and the foundry owner Adrien Hébrard judged that 74 could be cast in bronze. He influenced Jean-Louis Forain and Walter Sickert.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edgar Degas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In December 1873 Monet, Renoir, Pissarro, Sisley, Cézanne, Berthe Morisot, <b>Edgar Degas</b> and others founded the Société anonyme des artistes peintres, sculpteurs, graveurs to exhibit independently, its members forswearing the Salon.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1887" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edgar Degas"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1887">What was here in 1887? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
